+# [ARCHITECTURE_PLAN]
+
+## Topic
+
+Maintenance mode marking + enforcement. Plan file: `docs/arch/maintenance-mode.md`.
+
+Lets a developer mark individual route handlers, an entire controller, a WebSocket gateway, or an
+individual `@SubscribeMessage()` handler as "affected by maintenance mode". A runtime-toggleable
+flag, controllable from inside the running application (no redeploy), makes calls to exactly the
+marked targets fail fast with a clear error before any handler logic runs, while everything else
+keeps working. One mechanism covers both HTTP and WebSocket handlers by building entirely on
+Nest's existing transport-agnostic `CanActivate` guard extension point (already proven
+transport-agnostic by `packages/core/guards`, consumed identically by `packages/core/router` and
+`packages/websockets`), instead of inventing a second, transport-specific mechanism.
+
+## Implementation Order
+
+1. **packages/common/maintenance** — no Imports, pure marking/toggle primitives. Built first
+   because packages/core/maintenance depends on it.
+2. **packages/core/maintenance** — depends on packages/common/maintenance (for the metadata +
+   toggle) and packages/common/exceptions (for a typed rejection). Built second, after both
+   dependencies exist.
+
+Both cells are **new** (directories do not exist on disk yet).
+
+## Artifacts
+
+### Cell 1 (new): packages/common/maintenance
+
+```yaml
+Imports: []
+
+Usages:
+  testing: |
+    Unit tests must exercise `Maintenance()` metadata attachment and
+    `MaintenanceModeService` state transitions in isolation — no running
+    application, no HTTP server, no socket connection.
+
+Annotations: |
+  Provides the transport-agnostic marking and toggle primitives for maintenance
+  mode. `Maintenance()` attaches metadata to a controller class, a
+  `@WebSocketGateway()` class, or an individual handler method (HTTP route
+  handler or `@SubscribeMessage()` handler) — the same class-or-method shape
+  `@UseGuards()`/`SetMetadata` already use elsewhere in the framework, so one
+  decorator covers both "mark one handler" and "mark the whole
+  controller/gateway". `MaintenanceModeService` holds the single runtime
+  on/off flag; register it once (singleton scope) so any in-app admin action
+  that toggles it is immediately visible to the guard in
+  packages/core/maintenance that reads it.
+
+---
+
+"Maintenance() -> decorator: any":
+  location: maintenance-mode.decorator.ts
+  annotations: |
+    Marks a controller class, a `@WebSocketGateway()` class, or an individual
+    handler method (HTTP route handler or `@SubscribeMessage()` handler) as
+    affected by maintenance mode.
+
+    Implemented as a thin `SetMetadata` wrapper under a dedicated metadata
+    key, so a method-level marking and a class-level marking are both later
+    discoverable by a guard the same way `@UseGuards()`'s own metadata is.
+
+    Use `testing` for test requirements.
+
+"MaintenanceModeService()":
+  location: maintenance-mode.service.ts
+  annotations: |
+    Holds the single in-memory maintenance-mode flag for the application.
+    Register it once (default singleton provider scope) so the same instance
+    is toggled by an admin action and read by the guard.
+
+    Use `testing` for test requirements.
+  properties:
+    "enabled -> boolean": |
+      Current maintenance-mode state. `false` until `enable()` is called.
+  methods:
+    "enable()": |
+      Turns maintenance mode on. Calls to marked handlers are rejected from
+      this point until `disable()` is called.
+    "disable()": |
+      Turns maintenance mode off. Marked handlers resume normal execution.
+    "isEnabled() -> enabled: boolean": |
+      Returns the current maintenance-mode state.
+
+---
+
+Author: Goga
+CreatedAt: 16/09/26
+Description: |
+  Documents the transport-agnostic maintenance-mode marking primitives: the
+  `Maintenance()` decorator that flags a controller/gateway class or an
+  individual handler, and the `MaintenanceModeService` that holds the runtime
+  on/off toggle. packages/core/maintenance composes both with `Reflector` and
+  the guard extension point to actually reject marked handlers while the flag
+  is on.
+```
+
+### Cell 2 (new): packages/core/maintenance
+
+```yaml
+Imports:
+  - Types:
+      - Maintenance
+      - MaintenanceModeService
+    From: packages/common/maintenance
+  - Types:
+      - ServiceUnavailableException
+    From: packages/common/exceptions
+
+Usages:
+  testing: |
+    Unit tests must construct `MaintenanceModeGuard` directly with a mocked
+    `Reflector` (`{ getAllAndOverride: vi.fn() }`) and a real or mocked
+    `MaintenanceModeService`, plus a mocked `ExecutionContext` exposing
+    `getHandler()`/`getClass()`, then assert `canActivate()` for every
+    combination of (marked/unmarked) x (maintenance on/off) — no Nest
+    application instance, no HTTP server, no socket connection required.
+
+Annotations: |
+  Implements the actual rejection behind maintenance mode as one ordinary
+  `CanActivate` guard, reusing the same guard extension point documented in
+  packages/core/guards (`GuardsConsumer`/`GuardsContextCreator`) that
+  packages/core/router and packages/websockets already invoke identically for
+  HTTP routes and `@SubscribeMessage()` handlers. Registering this single
+  guard (globally via `APP_GUARD`, or locally via `@UseGuards()` on a
+  controller/gateway) is therefore the one mechanism that protects both
+  transports — no transport-specific branch is added here.
+
+---
+
+"MaintenanceModeGuard(reflector: any, maintenanceModeService: MaintenanceModeService)":
+  location: maintenance-mode.guard.ts
+  annotations: |
+    `reflector`: looks up the `Maintenance()` metadata for the current
+    target, checking the handler method first and falling back to its
+    declaring class (`getAllAndOverride`) — so both a single marked handler
+    and an entire marked controller/gateway are detected the same way.
+    `maintenanceModeService`: the runtime toggle from
+    packages/common/maintenance.
+
+    Use `testing` for test requirements.
+  methods:
+    "canActivate(context: any) -> allowed: boolean": |
+      Returns `true` immediately when the current handler/class is not
+      marked with `Maintenance()`, and when it is marked but
+      `maintenanceModeService.isEnabled()` is `false` — the handler's own
+      logic then runs normally.
+
+      When the target is marked AND maintenance mode is on, throws
+      `ServiceUnavailableException` before returning, so the handler's logic
+      never runs and the caller gets one clear, typed rejection instead of a
+      boolean `false`.
+
+---
+
+Author: Goga
+CreatedAt: 16/09/26
+Description: |
+  Documents the maintenance-mode enforcement guard: a `CanActivate`
+  implementation that composes the `Maintenance()` metadata and
+  `MaintenanceModeService` toggle from packages/common/maintenance with
+  `Reflector` to reject marked HTTP or WebSocket handlers before their logic
+  runs, while maintenance mode is on.
+```
+
+## Dependency Map
+
+```
+packages/common/maintenance  (Maintenance, MaintenanceModeService)
+        ^                                   ^
+        |                                   |
+        +----------------+  +---------------+
+                         |  |
+              packages/core/maintenance (MaintenanceModeGuard)
+                         |
+                         +--> packages/common/exceptions (ServiceUnavailableException)
+```
+
+`packages/core/maintenance` is consumed by application code the same way any other built-in guard
+is: registered via `@UseGuards(MaintenanceModeGuard)` or as an `APP_GUARD` provider. It does not
+introduce a new edge into `packages/core/guards`, `packages/core/router`, or `packages/websockets`
+— those cells' existing, already-documented guard plumbing (`GuardsConsumer`/
+`GuardsContextCreator`) picks it up unchanged because it is a standard `CanActivate`.
+
+## Verification Checklist
+
+- `packages/common/maintenance/CODEMANIFEST` and `packages/core/maintenance/CODEMANIFEST` both
+  pass `goga lint`.
+- `goga schema` lists both new cells with the dependency edges shown above.
+- `Maintenance()` attaches metadata reachable via `Reflector` both when applied to a method and
+  when applied to a class (verified in `maintenance-mode.decorator.spec.ts`).
+- `MaintenanceModeService.enable()/disable()/isEnabled()` round-trip correctly in isolation
+  (`maintenance-mode.service.spec.ts`).
+- `MaintenanceModeGuard.canActivate()` returns `true` for an unmarked target regardless of
+  maintenance state, `true` for a marked target while maintenance mode is off, and throws
+  `ServiceUnavailableException` for a marked target while maintenance mode is on — for both a
+  method-level marking and a class-level marking — using only mocked `Reflector`/
+  `ExecutionContext` objects, no real server or socket (`maintenance-mode.guard.spec.ts`).
